    "rules": [
      "VEX_CONFIG_DIR overrides every platform default, and ONLY when it is non-empty AND absolute for the TARGET platform. An accepted value is returned VERBATIM - no join, no clean, no trailing-separator normalisation - because the endpoint hash is taken over exactly these bytes.",
      "XDG_CONFIG_HOME (unix) and APPDATA (windows) are honoured under the SAME non-empty-and-absolute rule. Empty is treated as unset per the XDG Base Directory specification; relative is treated as unset so a typo cannot put privileged state in the launcher cwd.",
      "The platform default is joined from the home directory: unix ~/.config/vex, darwin ~/Library/Application Support/vex, windows ~/AppData/Roaming/vex. darwin does NOT consult XDG_CONFIG_HOME.",
      "Path flavour follows the TARGET platform, not the host running the resolver: windows joins with a backslash and normalises forward slashes; every other platform joins with a forward slash.",
      "LEXICAL NORMALISATION is part of the join, defined explicitly because the three implementations cannot each invent it: a JOINED platform default is normalised exactly as Node's path.posix.join / path.win32.join normalises - `.` segments dropped, `..` segments resolved against the preceding segment (and discarded at the root of an absolute path), repeated separators collapsed to one, a trailing separator on the base absorbed, forward slashes converted on win32, and a UNC (`\\\\\\\\server\\\\share`) or device (`C:`) root preserved rather than consumed by a `..`. The endpoint hash is taken over the RESULT, so `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=/home/alice/../vex-x` must resolve to `/home/vex-x/vex` on all three sides or the bridge dials a socket the app never bound. An accepted VEX_CONFIG_DIR is still returned VERBATIM and is NOT normalised; that asymmetry is pinned by its own cases."
    ],
